body:
Alright, I am 90% sure I figured out the issue. Due to memory alignment I think changes to the sky radiance are writing over the `directional_light_count` value which crashes the GPU driver

_edit: The following diff makes the issue go away:_

```
diff --git a/drivers/gles3/rasterizer_scene_gles3.cpp b/drivers/gles3/rasterizer_scene_gles3.cpp
index aa1b732b88..6700438136 100644
--- a/drivers/gles3/rasterizer_scene_gles3.cpp
+++ b/drivers/gles3/rasterizer_scene_gles3.cpp
@@ -1565,7 +1565,7 @@ void RasterizerSceneGLES3::_setup_environment(const RenderDataGLES3 *p_render_da

                        Basis sky_transform = environment_get_sky_orientation(p_render_data->environment);
                        sky_transform = sky_transform.inverse() * p_render_data->cam_transform.basis;
-                       GLES3::MaterialStorage::store_transform_3x3(sky_transform, scene_state.data.radiance_inverse_xform);
+                       //GLES3::MaterialStorage::store_transform_3x3(sky_transform, scene_state.data.radiance_inverse_xform);
                        scene_state.data.use_ambient_cubemap = (ambient_src == RS::ENV_AMBIENT_SOURCE_BG && env_bg == RS::ENV_BG_SKY) || ambient_src == RS::ENV_AMBIENT_SOURCE_SKY;
                        scene_state.data.use_ambient_light = scene_state.data.use_ambient_cubemap || ambient_src == RS::ENV_AMBIENT_SOURCE_COLOR;
                }
```

@BastiaanOlij I'll leave this to you to fix, But I suspect the proper solution is going to be to split the SceneDataBlock into 2 UBOs so you aren't having to manually manage the struct alignment rules. 

On the CPP side the current approach of asking for a chunk of data that is double the size of UBO and then passing in the pointer to only the first UBO is totally flawed. It ignores both GLSL alignment rules and CPP alignment rules. Each struct should be passed in on its own. 

On the GLSL side, we moved from a single uniform block with elements in it directly to a Uniform block with with two structs in it. This is super wasteful for anyone who isn't using motion vectors which is 99.999% of users. When motion vectors aren't enabled, there shouldn't be a trace of them in our code. This will take some refactoring of the scene.glsl file, but it needs to happen.
